/*!
    \example joints
    \ingroup quick3dphysics-examples
    \title Qt Quick 3D Physics - Joints Example
    \examplecategory {3D}
    \brief Demonstrates using joints in a physics scene.
    \image joints-example.png

    This example demonstrates using the joints available in Quick 3D Physics.
    Most joint types (\l{DistanceJoint}, \l{PrismaticJoint}, \l{RevoluteJoint},
    and \l{SphericalJoint}) inherit from \l{FlexibleJoint} to support soft constraints
    via stiffness and damping. Additionally, \l{D6Joint} provides fully configurable
    6-degrees-of-freedom constraints, while \l{FixedJoint} completely locks all degrees
    of freedom.

    The scene is a typical scene with a PhysicsWorld, a View3D with a
    PerspectiveCamera and a DirectionalLight:

    \snippet joints/main.qml full

    There is a StaticRigidBody with a PlaneShape to act as the floor. There are
    several custom QML objects (Rope, SoftRope, Spring, Prismatic, and Revolute)
    demonstrating different joint configurations.

    \snippet joints/main.qml joints

    The Rope object is a series of capsules and a sphere that are connected
    with \l{SphericalJoint}{spherical joints} for the capsules and a
    \l{FixedJoint}{fixed joint} for the sphere.

    \snippet joints/Rope.qml full

    The SoftRope object demonstrates how \l{FlexibleJoint}{flexible joints} use
    stiffness and damping properties to create spring-like soft connections.

    \snippet joints/SoftRope.qml full

    The Spring object shows how to use \l{D6Joint} to constrain motion across 6 degrees
    of freedom with custom linear and angular spring parameters.

    \snippet joints/Spring.qml full

    The Prismatic object consists of two bars locked in a
    \l{PrismaticJoint}{prismatic joint} along the x-axis of both of them.
    With this joint the smaller rod can move freely along this x-axis until it
    reaches its \l{PrismaticJoint::upperLimit}{upper} and
    \l{PrismaticJoint::lowerLimit}{lower} constraint limit.

    \snippet joints/Prismatic.qml full

    The Revolute object consists of two bars rotated by 90 degrees and locked
    in a \l{RevoluteJoint}{revolute joint}.

    \snippet joints/Revolute.qml full

    Going back to the main scene there is a FrameAnimation object that rotates
    the kinematic \l{PrismaticJoint}{prismatic} and \l{RevoluteJoint}{revolute}
    joints.

    \snippet joints/main.qml animation

    With all these moving parts we can see how the different joints interact in
    a physics scene.
*/

